    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $\\mathbf{B}$ represent the number of gold coins in the box.\n\nWe are given that $\\mathbf B \\equiv 4 \\mod 6$ and $\\mathbf B \\equiv 3 \\mod 5$. We notice that if we add 5 to $\\mathbf B$, we can form a multiple of 5. Therefore, $\\mathbf B \\equiv 5 \\mod 6$.\n\nSince both $\\mathbf B \\equiv 4 \\mod 6$ and $\\mathbf B \\equiv 5 \\mod 6$ are true, therefore $\\mathbf B \\equiv \\boxed{11}$\n\nTherefore, when the gold coins are equally divided among seven people, there are $\\boxed{5}$ coins left.",
