    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Method1\nWe need to find the smallest number of gold coins that leaves a remainder of 4 when divided by 6 and a remainder of 3 when divided by 5.\nThis is a word problem:\n-Lisa is dividing her coins equally among her 5 friends.\n- Aaron's test score leaves a remainder of 3.\n- Suppose there are fewer than 100 coins in the store.\n\nTo solve this, find the numbers that meet both conditions below:\n- What number of coins leaves a remainder of 4 when divided by 6?\n- What number of coins leaves a remainder of 3 when divided by 5?\n\nThese are the various calculations we would perform:\n-4 x 6 = - 24 (providing 4 as a remainder)\n= 6 times -4 gives a remainder of 4\n\n-3 x 5 = - 15 (providing 3 the remainder)\n= 5 times -3 gives a remainder of 3\n\nThese indicate that 3 and 6 are the numbers that meet these two conditions.\n\nTherefore, the coin is 3 x 6 = 18 (coins)\nDividing them equally among seven people, the remainder is 3 x 5 = 15 (coins)\nIn this calculation, we use for calculating 15 the value of the first number multiplied by the second number. This is because we have an expression (3 x 5) to be solved. Thus,\n-15:3 represents the remainder after calculating the number of people to be divided by.\n-15:7 represented by the number of people to evenly divide the number of a single person.\n\nPutting it:\nWe have to find the smallest number of coins that meets two requirements:\n-When divided by 5, the remainder must be 3.\n-When divided by 6, the remainder must be 4.\n\nSetting up the expressions, we have:\nSmallest number = (3 x 5) + 6 : 6\nSmallest number = 15 + 6 : 6\nSmallest number = 21\nTherefore, Minimo%q%A%cants are 21 (hamos).\n\nWhen the coins are divided among seven people, there will be 15 (coins) left.\n\nMethod 2:\nFinal answer:\nNumber 18.",
